---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { url, formatDate, CORRECTION_URL, CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO_URL } from '../lib/site';

const amendments = (await getCollection('amendments')).map((a) => a.data).sort((a, b) => a.number - b.number);
const races = (await getCollection('races')).map((r) => r.data).filter((r) => r.research_status === 'complete');
---

<Base title="How this guide works" description="Why arguments are shown without labels, how they were gathered, and the sourcing and ordering rules this guide follows.">
  <h1>How this guide works</h1>
  <p class="lede">The idea: let every argument stand on its own.</p>

  <h2>Why arguments are unlabeled</h2>
  <p>
    Most voter guides tell you who is making an argument — a party, a campaign, an advocacy group. That is useful
    information, but it also invites readers to accept or reject an argument based on who said it rather than what it
    says. This guide shows each argument as plain text, with no party, ideology, organization, or person attached, so
    you can weigh it on its merits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is hidden: every argument has a numbered footnote, and the collapsed <strong>Sources</strong> section at the
    bottom of each page lists where it came from. You can look; you just aren't nudged by default.
  </p>

  <h2>How arguments were gathered</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>
      Arguments were collected from across the political spectrum: candidates' campaign materials, party and
      legislative statements, recorded legislative debate as reported, advocacy organizations, independent candidates,
      and established news outlets reporting what supporters and opponents say.
    </li>
    <li>Each argument is paraphrased in plain language and stated in its strongest fair form, as its supporters would make it.</li>
    <li>
      Wording that identifies a side ("As conservatives…", "Progressives believe…") is rewritten as a plain claim. The
      build fails if any argument contains a party or ideology term from a <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/src/data/config/blocklist.yaml`}>published blocklist</a>.
    </li>
    <li>When different sources make the same argument, it appears once with several footnotes.</li>
    <li>Arguments are never invented to fill a gap. If none was found, the page says "No published arguments found" with the date checked.</li>
    <li>Direct quotes are short and never attributed to a named person in the text.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Candidates' platforms and reasons for running</h2>
  <p>
    "Why they're running" and "Platform" describe each candidate's <em>own</em> stated positions, taken only from their
    campaign websites, announcements, questionnaires they answered, and interviews where they speak directly. What
    opponents say about a candidate goes under "Arguments against," never under "Platform." Planks are paraphrased
    neutrally, capped at eight per candidate, and grouped into shared issue categories so you can compare candidates
    side by side. Where a candidate has not addressed a category, the compare table says "No stated position."
    Positions are never inferred from party.
  </p>
  <p>
    A candidate's party appears only because it is printed on the official ballot, styled identically for every party.
    Candidates are listed in the order of the Secretary of State's certified candidate list.
  </p>

  <h2>Sourcing rules</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>Every argument, platform plank, and reason for running has at least one source URL. Content without a source fails the build and does not publish.</li>
    <li>
      Official text, ballot language, summaries, fiscal notes, and legislative votes in the "Just the facts" blocks come
      from government sources: the Secretary of State and the Tennessee General Assembly (capitol.tn.gov).
    </li>
    <li>Government sites don't publish pros and cons, so arguments come from the non-government sources listed above.</li>
    <li>Every source link is checked automatically when the site is built.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ordering rule</h2>
  <p>
    Order can suggest importance, so arguments within each column are shuffled using a fixed seed for each item when
    the site is built. The order is effectively random but stays the same between visits. On small screens the two
    columns become tabs; the first tab shown is always <strong>YES</strong> for amendments and
    <strong>Arguments for</strong> for candidates. Both columns get the same styling and space.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide doesn't do</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>It doesn't tell you how to vote, rate candidates, or score positions.</li>
    <li>It doesn't use candidate photos or party logos.</li>
    <li>It isn't affiliated with any party, campaign, candidate, or government agency.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Coverage and last-updated dates</h2>
  <p>Fully researched so far:</p>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    {amendments.map((a) => <li><a href={url(`amendments/${a.id}`)}>Amendment {a.number}: {a.title}</a> — verified {formatDate(a.last_verified)}</li>)}
    {races.map((r) => <li><a href={url(`races/${r.id}`)}>{r.office}</a> — verified {formatDate(r.last_verified)}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>
    U.S. House, state Senate, and state House pages currently list the certified candidates from the Secretary of
    State. Platforms and arguments for those races are being added.
  </p>

  <h2>Suggest a correction</h2>
  <p>
    Found an error, a missing argument, or a candidate's published platform we haven't included?
    Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or <a href={CORRECTION_URL}>open a correction
    request</a> on GitHub. A source link is required, and the same rules
    apply: arguments are added without labels, and platforms come only from the candidate's own materials.
  </p>
</Base>
